The approach is a direct implementation of the barycentric sign test. The orientation function computes twice the signed area of the triangle formed by three points, using the shoelace determinant, so a positive result says those points turn counterclockwise. A point inside the triangle has the same orientation with each edge as the third vertex. A point on an edge has one orientation of zero; within the pairwise clamp above, I then require one actual equality with a signed pair, which distinguishes a genuine edge from the three-collinear case that would otherwise report True by mistake. All torsion leaves.

