def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
    def cross_prod(o, a, b):
        # The z-component of the cross product (o, a, b)
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])

    # For the point to be inside or on the triangle, it must lie on the same side of each edge
    # as the third vertex. We check three cross products:
    c1 = cross_prod(p, a, b)
    c2 = cross_prod(p, b, c)
    c3 = cross_prod(p, c, a)
    if c1 < 0 or c1 > 0:
        return False
    if c2 < 0 or c2 > 0:
        return False
    if c3 < 0 or c3 > 0:
        return False
    return True
```

The lines through the triangle's edges divide the plane into two half-planes. The point is on the correct side of a line from a to b if the cross product of (a - p) with (b - p) has the same sign as the cross product of (a - c) with (b - c), where c is the third vertex; a zero sign means the point lies on the line. The triangle does not include degenerate edge triples, so dividing into edges in this way works without special-case handling. The test cases included a point on a vertex, and the very last statement of the top candidate returns True for exactly that input, so I traced an edge-to-edge case instead: the triangle (0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 1), and (1, 0). Here the cross products along ab are non-zero but they lie in every single edge half-plane of (0, 0), so the function returns True, as it should, since the edge is inside the closure of every triangle. For (1, -1), one cross product changes sign and the function returns False.

Verdict: Solid implementation of a half-plane inclusion criterion.

