def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])
    c1 = cross(a, b, p)
    c2 = cross(b, c, p)
    c3 = cross(c, a, p)
    return c1 >= 0 and c2 >= 0 and c3 >= 0
```

The function decides whether the point lies inside or exactly on the triangle by comparing its orientation with respect to each of the triangle's three edges. Given three points o, a and b, (a[0]−o[0])(b[1]−o[1])−(a[1]−o[1])(b[0]−o[0]) equals twice the signed area of the triangle they form, so it is positive when a turns left from o to b, zero when the three points are collinear, and negative otherwise. The point p lies inside the triangle or on an edge exactly when it is on the same side of each edge as the triangle's third vertex, which is equivalent to the three values from this computation all being non-negative. That also covers the degenerate case in which the point is one of the vertices.

