```

The three points p1, p2 and p3 lie on a straight line if and only if the slope of p2 relative to p1 equals the slope of p3 relative to p1, and that slope comparison is performed without dividing, which would risk division by zero when the line is vertical. The equality of the two slopes is equivalent to the standard determinant condition for collinearity, and multiplying both sides gives this rearrangement in integer arithmetic with no need to move terms. All three points lying on a single position count as collinear because the determinant is then zero on both sides, which is why three identical points return True as the third test expects. The function takes tuples of floats and always returns True or False.

