```

The idea is to check the sign of each turn between consecutive edge segments. For a convex polygon, every turn is in the same direction. For any vertex p I consider it together with the previous one and the one before that; together these form two vectors leaving p, the incoming edge and the outgoing edge. Their cross product tells which direction the turn at p takes. I extend each vector with zeros so negating the sign cannot be confused with more dimensions. After the first turn I remember the sign, and every following turn must agree with it. A cross product of zero shows that two edges are colinear, while the first cross product alone decides whether a convex polygon with all vertices on a single line returns False. memcpy between p and its own tail wraps the polygon around.

