def is_convex_polygon(points):
    n = len(points)
    if n < 3:
        return False
    for i in range(n):
        a, b, c = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n], points[(i + 2) % n]
        cross = (b[0] - a[0]) * (c[1] - a[1]) - (b[1] - a[1]) * (c[0] - a[0])
        if cross == 0:
            return False
        sign = cross > 0
        for j in range(n - 2):
            x = points[(i + 2 + j) % n]
            y = points[(i + 3 + j) % n]
            cross = (x[0] - a[0]) * (y[1] - a[1]) - (x[1] - a[1]) * (y[0] - a[0])
            if cross == 0:
                return False
            if (cross > 0) != sign:
                return False
    return True

    for i in range(n):
        a, b, c = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n], points[(i + 2) % n]
        cross = det(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1], c[0] - a[0], c[1] - a[1])
        if cross == 0:
            return False
        for j in range(i + 2, n + i):
            cross = det(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1], points[j % n][0] - a[0], points[j % n][1] - a[1])
            if cross == 0:
                return False
            if (cross > 0) != (cross > 0):
                return False
    return True
```

For a polygon to be convex, every interior angle must be strictly less than 180 degrees, so I test the sign of the cross product of every pair of consecutive edge direction vectors from a single point. If the sign ever changes, the turn between edges reverses direction and the polygon is not convex. The same point can be considered as the tail of every edge, so each point starts an n - 2 tests. To stop a collinear input from slipping through, I include any vertex that lies on the same line through two of its neighbours; they must also receive the sign test from a later edge, since otherwise the first such pair could both bear the same sign. The result is False when all the points sit on a line and only True when every turn is consistently signed.

